// The game's WON front end, EEWonNet, R6 WON round 31 made whole Rust (ee_won_lobby wonnet.rs) against the deleted
// C++ as it was (won_wonnet_orig.h, generated by script from the round's base), over one stand-in world
// (won_wonnet_world.h) that logs every effect. The Rust's calls out are answered by the host's very statements
// (ee/EEWonNetHost.inl, included here as EEWonNet.cpp includes it) over the same stand-ins. Each sequence picks the
// world's answers (the routing groups and clients, the forms and checkboxes, the rooms box, the file, the key) and
// runs one to twelve steps: a method with arguments drawn from small sets, or the completion of a pending request
// with a drawn op (status, ids, records, data). The two sides' traces, the objects' data after each step (the Rust's
// dump against the same text made from the original's members) and the readers' answers must be the same. The exit
// code is the number of mismatching sequences.
